2186045417 true] [17592186092261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562394 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186092263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562416 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186096635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562476 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186096635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562489 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186096636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562428 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186096636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562508 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186096637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562491 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562478 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562488 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562400 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562457 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562447 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562499 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562466 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562505 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186105950 1050 420.0 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186107080 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186107080 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186107097 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186107097 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186108673 1078 #edb/ref 17592186108674 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186110755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562479 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186110792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562415 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186111158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186111158 1071 "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin θ$." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186111158 1071 "![](images/d5/d51a8e78f8b8c1f05e502bd98aed6a253799336765f1331d23e63ec637bcb448.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186111158 1074 "![](images/43/438ce908c902696b480bf9791cae089be40f9e7b2552208e13e08405496b696c.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186112692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112695 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186112692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186112696 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186112692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561964 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186112692 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562003 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186112692 1078 #edb/ref 17592186112697 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186115261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562496 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186115274 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562439 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186115621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186115621 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. Which of the following points must lie on $y = f(x + 2) - 2$?" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186115621 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(1,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x+2)-2?$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186115621 1074 "The graph of $y=f(x+2)-2$  is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the *left* and $2$ units down. So the $y$-coordinate decreases by $2$ and the $x$-coordinate decreases by $2.$\n\nWe know that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(1-2,-1-2)=(-1,-3)$ must lie on $y=f(x+2)-2.$\n\n![](images/89/89f839666dc5471bfe43064d01c9c4f5c60a9ac4bfc8abe8cf83ac6d6592bf42.png)" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186117993 1078 #edb/ref 17592186117998 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186119805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562406 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186119805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562438 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186119805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562512 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186119806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562449 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186119807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562514 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186120309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186120309 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186120309 1071 "Consider the vectors\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf $x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} = b$, then what is $x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}$?" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186120309 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-3$ and from the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3+2(-3)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ which means \n\n$$\n11{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}-3{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=11+3-3=11.\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121797 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121798 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121799 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121800 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186121801 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561684 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561782 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561984 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562049 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562142 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186121796 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561984 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122181 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122182 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122183 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122184 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122185 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561632 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561775 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562017 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562121 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562210 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186122180 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561775 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186124022 1075 #edb/ref 17592186124023 13194139536054 17592186045417