}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130339 1064 "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/10/1074870d255109821b5f36eb517f22531efbd8f5069f05c3de3f111b38befd02.svg)\n\nThe first non-zero entry on each row (from left to right) is called the **leading entry** of that row.\n\n- In the matrix $A,$ the leading entries are $a_{11}=2,$ $a_{22}=3,$ $a_{32}=1,$ and $a_{43}=5.$\n\n- In the matrix $B,$ the leading entries are $b_{11}=1,$ $b_{22}=3,$ $b_{33}=-2,$ and $b_{44}=2.$ The $5$th row has no leading entry.\n\nThe leading entries are highlighted below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b1d99269c847f7c6bc0558da347fb300dba5fa6caf373cd1ed5cc5ebacb1649.svg)" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130353 1064 "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/06/06a28b9b9d8db65a4214ed1d446c68f288978acc59afd3720873da7f825efe13.svg)\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n![](images/1b/1b7aa45404b3e6e02cb9802da371f6c74f6848f4b5601261f2257e12b7d362d0.svg)\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n![](images/0f/0f29362b05dd18b6a8a307f531cea6d59c083301703285bde41a9488f228c386.svg)\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n![](images/d1/d1b2efc5b22e34c0bb4cb93a4777c8d8139ca0047253b7177bd1809a272f5b37.svg)\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130353 1064 "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\text{i} \\\\ -1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130354 1064 "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n\n$$\nA = PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n*where*\n![](images/74/74b63ce33cfa7779a6668a22974b709d549843a221c3b13067887e8193c6e6f0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130354 1064 "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n> *Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A = PCP^{-1}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}\n> $$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130355 1064 "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/74/74c17c58d6b39ff6a13ec56e135ec17ce0f260d2e9d90e562544637f6e677db0.svg)\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n![](images/15/1542ac2d3f79e1494ecf9e67dda3219966efdf76267ebda82d7689555dc4df30.svg)\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n![](images/f0/f0d562472e2b230a0cd393829ab4a5dec380e6fd1e2a797af4f1f651aae88f75.svg)\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130355 1064 "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}+1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130356 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **block diagonalizable** if it is similar to a block diagonal matrix $B.$ That is, there exists a block diagonal matrix $B$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/18/1857eb7b5b094ae9cdb3c233be3513e1c9a725d68d104145db573f02be065340.svg) \n\nis block diagonalizable because it can be factored as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43f25f96d39e392d7b1b0910eee54650e6b3c1a977c0cc43c4c04aebe2f4b085.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $A$ has eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1 = 2},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2=3-2\\textrm i},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3=3+2\\textrm i}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda_3}.$\nThe eigenvectors of ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3},$ respectively, are\n\n![](images/a9/a91e32ad98d0b2bf93053745873fd219c2ed6fbd533c3e8a974032d63feea161.svg)\n\nAgain, notice that $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}_3}.$\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1},$ and the second and third columns correspond to the real and the imaginary parts of ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130356 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **block diagonalizable** if it is similar to a block diagonal matrix $B.$ That is, there exists a block diagonal matrix $B$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis block diagonalizable because it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the matrix $A$ has eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1 = 2},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2=3-2\\textrm i},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3=3+2\\textrm i}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda_3}.$\nThe eigenvectors of ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3},$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, notice that $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}_3}.$\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1},$ and the second and third columns correspond to the real and the imaginary parts of ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130357 1064 "We have the following theorem regarding the block diagonalization of a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix:\n\n*If $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then* \n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1},\n$$\n\n*where the matrices $B$ and $P$ are given by*\n![](images/97/97adc950e68b3e7bce042b741e7b0a33f4baa2f9aae91f9bb40b4bbe36b0b595.svg)\n\nIn other words, if $A$ is a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue and two complex eigenvalues, then $A$ is block diagonalizable.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The block of $B$ constructed from the real and imaginary parts of $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We can write the blocks of $B$ in either order. However, the order of the columns of $P$ *must* correspond to the order of the blocks." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130357 1064 "We have the following theorem regarding the block diagonalization of a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix:\n\n> *If $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A=PBP^{-1},\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where the matrices $B$ and $P$ are given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n\nIn other words, if $A$ is a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue and two complex eigenvalues, then $A$ is block diagonalizable.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The block of $B$ constructed from the real and imaginary parts of $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We can write the blocks of $B$ in either order. However, the order of the columns of $P$ *must* correspond to the order of the blocks." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186130380 1064 "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n![](images/af/af61f32297e248ab9989ba59ea6c9bda2f99efe19d25df525fef916617843a32.svg)\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186130380 1064 "Let's consider our matrices $A$ and $B$ once more, with the leading entries highlighted.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} & 6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 6 & 9 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{red}1}} & 4 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{5} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{1} & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} & 4 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-2} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe say that a matrix is in **row echelon form** if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nHere, $B$ is in row echelon form since both conditions are satisfied. However, $A$ is not in row echelon form since condition II is violated: the leading entry of the third row $(a_{32}={\\color{red}1})$ is placed just below the leading entry of the second row $(a_{22}=3).$\n\nGenerally speaking, row echelon form means that we have *stairs* where each step (represented by a leading entry $\\bbox[lightgray, 3pt]{\\large\\ast}$) has a height of $1.$\n\n![](images/67/67f83c454e59d16ae80cf67a19b19f968182251a2e5d1b405e2c422e0313d5b7.png)\n\nFinally, an *augmented* matrix is in row echelon form if the coefficient matrix (i.e., the part that is to the left of the vertical bar) is in row echelon form." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186133825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562513 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186138411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562440 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186138411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562481 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186138412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562453 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186147475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562474 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186147576 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562391 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186151763 1078 #edb/ref 17592186151764 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152143 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562417 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562418 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562419 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562515 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562420 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562473 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152221 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562502 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562414 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562429 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562451 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152222 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562468 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152876 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n1. $ℓ^{2}$, the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n2. $R_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n3. $M_{100 \\cdot 1000}(R)$, the vector space of all $100 \\cdot 1000$ matrices over $R$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186152876 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{2},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the squares of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R),$ the vector space of all $100\\times 1\\,000$ matrices over $R$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186152876 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{2}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{2}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{2}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{2}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{100\\times 1000}(R))=100\\cdot 1\\,000=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186157269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562433 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186157270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562436 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186157271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562471 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186164671 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164676 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186164677 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164682 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186164683 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164687 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186164688 1078 #edb/ref 17592186164691 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186166927 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562421 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186166928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562503 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562279 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562294 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562296 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-4x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186167254 1071 "${x^{′} = 2x(1 - 4x) - xy; y^{′} =-2y + 4xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium $x^{∗} = [0, 0]^{T}$, we get\n\n| $J(x^{∗}) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $$. |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186167254 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-4x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=2-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-4x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-16x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-16(0)-(0) & -(0) \\\\ 4(0) & -2+4(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186179762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562486 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186185784 1078 #edb/ref 17592186185786 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562413 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562441 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562483 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186562490 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188584 1071 "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 3, 3]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ given that $f(-1) = 2$." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186188584 1071 "![](images/9d/9d3726847858c1afc66497820a48fa6d6bbe39e4d909b84b562a2daeea085bb4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-1)=2.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186188584 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function comprises $2$ triangles, $2$ rectangles, and $1$ trapezoid. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/99/99f558b3c1d86c5638543361a63823f13a9e01f2cc5042d0f781080f9f33c8a2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{3}{2}+1) \\\\  & =2-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+2+4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{19}{2}\\}=\\frac{19}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{19}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186233646 1071 "If ${e}^{2x+3}=7,$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186233646 1071 "If ${e}^{2x+3}=7,$ then $x≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186233646 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx -0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186233646 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186311146 1075 #edb/ref 17592186561698 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186311146 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309307 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186311146 1078 #edb/ref 17592186561698 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186335786 1071 "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186335786 1071 "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186335786 1074 "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186335786 1074 "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 false] [17592186397863 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{20}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{20}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139536054 17592186045417 true] [17592186397863 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,